A||||8||theogre||All Users Please Read. Copy Rights.||||1||
Z||000000||theogre||08-29-2000||12:18 PM||||I've noticed some people pasting some or all the text of other web pages, etc, into forum responces. Please DON'T do that. <P>That material is nearly all copyrighted. Pasting it here could cause problems for Cliff if the owner of it gets peaved. Same goes for any printed material that you may have scanned/typed in. (This is why I can't copy any of the GM TSBs & recall documents to the cave.)<P>It's OK to post links but please don't copy and paste pages from other sites. <P>Most of the readers of this sites are in WIPO member countries. That means the copy rights of U.S. documents are recognized nearly world wide and we recognize the other members. (WIPO, World Intelectual Property Org.) This makes it an even uglier mess if a copyright holder has a cow. The whole thing ends up in international and/or U.S. federal courts.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||08-29-2000||06:08 PM||||You can still use the info if you paraphrase it though, right?  You're just referring to an exact copy?  Does a copyright ever run out?  Seems like I remember that the rights to a patent run out after a certain length of time (7 years?).  If a copyright does run out and it's not renewed, would it be OK to use the material then?||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-29-2000||08:24 PM||||I'm talking about stuff that was cut and paste from another site. Lately there have been several whole pages copied. That is a problem.<P>Paraphrased stuff generally shouldn't be an issue as long as it's more you than the original. <P>Copyrights are differant worldwide. In the U.S. it depends on a few things if or when it ever runs out. <P>As I understand them.... <P>Copyrights held by comercial entities almost never expire. They are usually bought and sold like all other company property.<P>Individual rights expire at the death of the author unless the author assigns the rights to another individual, a company, or an estate. <P>This DOSN'T take into account the Millenium Digital Copyright Act passed last year. This thing is screwing with the whole system. It's caused allot of problems and issues over things that traditionally fall under "Fair Use"<P>An example of fair use is making a copy of a CD <I>that you own</I> so you can play it in your cassette only car. (It's Piracy when you don't own the CD or you own the CD but give the copy to a friend.) Another example of fair use is copying your Haynes book so you don't trash it outside working on the car.<P>There's a whole huge raft of stuff about this on <A HREF="http://www.loc.gov/" TARGET=_blank>the Library of Congress</A> web site. That's probly the best place to learn more.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||RobertVol||08-29-2000||11:47 PM||||Agreed on all points. Some may think this is crazy but I always check with the site owner before copying their main sign on their website to my website for link purposes just because its respectful and because its their document or picture or graphic. As always.......check first ! good to always CYA!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||08-30-2000||02:46 AM||||Ogre, How does it apply to "out of print" material? As an example I have a "Pontiac Performance Plus" magazine. Was printed in 1985 by Hot Rod Magazine for Pontiac. The magazine is no long in print. I am in the process of scanning the pages onto a CD-ROM for preservation but many members have expressed an intrest in a copy. I did not plan on "selling" it just sending out copies.<BR>From what you say I can copy it to CD for my own use but what about friends and neighbors?<BR>Do I have to get permission from the publisher even though it's out of print?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mattsfiero||08-30-2000||08:14 AM||||If it is on the web then it is there for our use hence why one can copy exactly what is on any web page....90% of all meterial used here on the forum is from another fiero site. Cliff will not have any problems with these pages being posted and if he feels as such then he will say and we can stop however I have not seen any copyright infringements on this forum yet.<P>Matt||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnsFiero||08-30-2000||01:24 PM||||Just because something is on the internet does not make it public domain.  Any original written material is protected by copyright laws whether that copyright is expressed on a webpage or not.  The library is also open to the public but you cannot take a book and pass it off as being your original work.  <P>I think Ogre has a very valid point.  Give credit where credit is due for somebody else's hard work. When students copy from a website or a book verbatim without proper referencing it is called plagerism, as it is on a bulletin board.  Just my two cents<P>John      ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-30-2000||03:24 PM||||Oreif, In/Out of print doesn't make a diferance. Pontiac or Hot Rod still owns the rights to it. Making a copy for your own use would fall under "fair use." (Keep the original some place safe.) Giving/selling copies to friends is a no no.<P>Mattsfiero, John is correct Web publication does not equal public domain. Web pages don't need a copyright notice on them to still be protected. If we follow what you've presented then the Pontiac Logo would be public domain and as several lawsuits have shown GM's lawyers have proven it's not.||||reg||1
Z||000008||wkayl||08-30-2000||03:39 PM||||I have to admit that I have cut and pasted stuff in the past.  I never even gave it a thought.  From now on if any one has a question that I can answer with what I have found elseware, I will just link the site.||||reg||1
